examples.explanation:
`typeof` is JavaScript: it looks at the value while the program runs, and `12.5` is a number. `process.features.typescript` is `"strip"`, because Node 26 handles TypeScript by stripping the types. This is what Node actually runs, with the annotation replaced by spaces. It is plain JavaScript and prints the same two lines:

```typescript
const price         = 12.5;

console.log(typeof price);
console.log(process.features.typescript);
```

No annotation is left at runtime, so nothing there can check one. That job belongs to `tsc`, before the program starts.

