  }
}

const mug = new Product("Mug", 12.99);
console.log(`${mug.name} costs $${mug.price}`);

examples.explanation:
Each field is declared in the class body with its type, then the constructor assigns it, the way plain JavaScript classes work. Many codebases use parameter properties instead, a shorthand where a modifier on a constructor parameter declares and assigns the field at once. Run this version:

```typescript error
class Product {
  constructor(public name: string, public price: number) {}
}

console.log(new Product("Mug", 12.99).name);
```

`tsc` reports TS1294 at both parameters, because the shorthand needs generated code. Modifiers like `public` and `private` are fine on a declared field, where they are only types and Node erases them.

